(Bug #34 - a float at or beyond 2^63 printed as a saturated i64:
 9223372036854775808.372036854775808 for every such magnitude, because
 the formatter routed the integer part through cvttsd2si, which
 saturates past i64::MAX. The stored value was always correct - dividing
 the "broken" value by two proved it - only the print path was wrong.

 `over`, `negover`, and `atboundary` are the bug rows: values at or past
 2^63 must print their own exact digits, not the saturated constant, and
 must agree across Print, "{x}" interpolation, and `x as text`, since
 all three funnel into the same formatter.

 `belowboundary`, `one18`, `half`, and `roundctrl` are controls that
 must keep passing on both sides of the fix: everything under 2^63 was
 already correct (cvttsd2si never saturates there), including the case
 where a double's own IEEE-754 rounding legitimately changes the last
 few digits of a huge literal - that is correct behaviour, not this bug.)

a float called over is 10000000000000000000.0.
a float called negover is 0.0 subtract over.
a float called atboundary is 9223372036854775808.0.
a float called belowboundary is 9223372036854773760.0.
a float called one18 is 1000000000000000000.0.
a float called half is over divide 2.0.
a float called roundctrl is 1243626351836374761.1224435542121323.

Print over.
Print negover.
Print atboundary.
Print belowboundary.
Print one18.
Print half.
Print roundctrl.

Print "interpolated {over}".
Print over as text.
